WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.790
When researchers use AI to
help code qualitative data,

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.741
they usually report one
result, from one setup.

00:00:08.121 --> 00:00:12.595
This video explains why that result is
only one of many that were possible,

00:00:12.595 --> 00:00:16.055
and what happened when 32
setups were tested side by side.

00:00:17.555 --> 00:00:21.606
Qualitative coding means reading
text, such as interviews or reports,

00:00:21.606 --> 00:00:25.188
and labelling passages with
codes that capture what they say.

00:00:25.568 --> 00:00:29.808
In directed content analysis, the
codes are not invented along the way.

00:00:29.808 --> 00:00:32.616
They come from an existing
theory or framework,

00:00:32.616 --> 00:00:34.885
written down in advance as a codebook.

00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:39.059
A trained coder reads each
document, decides which codes apply,

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:42.070
and marks the passages
that support each decision.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.535
Large language models,
the technology behind AI

00:00:46.535 --> 00:00:49.499
chatbots, can now do a
first pass of this work.

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:53.456
The model is given the codebook,
a document, and instructions,

00:00:53.456 --> 00:00:57.265
and it returns the codes it thinks
apply, with quotes as evidence.

00:00:57.645 --> 00:01:01.082
To check its work, researchers
compare the AI's codes with

00:01:01.082 --> 00:01:04.876
codes from trained human coders,
and report how well they agree.

00:01:06.376 --> 00:01:11.093
This study measures agreement with the
Matthews correlation coefficient, or MCC.

00:01:11.473 --> 00:01:15.246
Think of it as a score where 0
means the AI matches the human

00:01:15.246 --> 00:01:18.958
coders no better than chance,
and 1 means perfect agreement.

00:01:19.338 --> 00:01:23.146
It counts both kinds of mistake:
codes the AI applied that the

00:01:23.146 --> 00:01:27.077
humans did not, and codes the
humans applied that the AI missed.

00:01:28.577 --> 00:01:31.809
However, every reported score
rests on a stack of choices.

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:36.811
How much text does the model read at once?
Is the codebook sent whole, or in parts?

00:01:36.811 --> 00:01:41.433
How are the instructions worded? Which
model is used, and how hard should it think?

00:01:41.813 --> 00:01:43.698
Then, when the results are scored:

00:01:43.698 --> 00:01:47.359
whose coding counts as the reference,
and which statistic is used?

00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:52.150
Each of these choices is defensible,
and few have a single right answer.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:57.296
With three options at each of nine choices,
there are nearly 20,000 possible setups.

00:01:57.676 --> 00:02:00.475
Every published result is
one path through this maze.

00:02:01.975 --> 00:02:04.958
Researchers in psychology
met a similar problem,

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:07.257
known as the garden of forking paths:

00:02:07.257 --> 00:02:11.980
with many reasonable choices, analysts
can arrive at very different results.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.807
One answer was multiverse analysis.
Rather than reporting a single path,

00:02:16.807 --> 00:02:21.316
you run the defensible alternatives
and report how much the results move.

00:02:21.696 --> 00:02:25.771
The preprint adapts this idea to
AI-assisted qualitative coding.

00:02:27.271 --> 00:02:31.199
To see which choices matter,
the study used a public dataset:

00:02:31.199 --> 00:02:33.711
40 reports of smoking-cessation trials,

00:02:33.711 --> 00:02:37.059
already coded by trained
annotators for 94 behaviour

00:02:37.059 --> 00:02:40.794
change techniques, such as
goal setting or social support.

00:02:41.174 --> 00:02:45.611
Two low-cost models, Claude
Haiku 5.5 and GPT-6 Luna,

00:02:45.611 --> 00:02:48.876
coded the reports under 16 setups each.

00:02:49.256 --> 00:02:52.469
The setups varied five
choices: asking the model to

00:02:52.469 --> 00:02:55.619
find relevant passages or
to decide on every code;

00:02:55.619 --> 00:03:00.282
sending the codebook whole or in five
parts; low or high reasoning effort;

00:03:00.282 --> 00:03:04.125
two wordings of the instructions;
and the order of the codes.

00:03:04.505 --> 00:03:07.272
One setup per model was
also run four times,

00:03:07.272 --> 00:03:10.480
to see how much the results
change by chance alone.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.559
Two open-source tools made this practical:

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:16.387
QualFrame records every
choice as a setting,

00:03:16.387 --> 00:03:21.143
and QualBench scores every run. The
whole screen cost about 14 US dollars.

00:03:22.643 --> 00:03:28.134
Here is what the study found.
First, setup choices mattered.

00:03:28.134 --> 00:03:32.801
Across the 32 setups, MCC
ranged from 0.24 to 0.57.

00:03:33.181 --> 00:03:36.625
The same models, reading the same
reports with the same codebook,

00:03:36.625 --> 00:03:40.387
could look much worse or much better
depending on how they were set up.

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:43.588
The seven best setups all
asked the model to decide

00:03:43.588 --> 00:03:46.463
on every code, with the
whole codebook sent at once.

00:03:46.843 --> 00:03:51.017
And on average, the instructions, the
reasoning effort, and how the codebook was

00:03:51.017 --> 00:03:55.035
divided each made a bigger difference
than the choice between the two models.

00:03:56.535 --> 00:03:59.511
Second, a stable score
can hide unstable coding.

00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:03.580
When one setup was run
four times, the four scores

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.047
were almost identical,
varying by 0.02 or less.

00:04:07.427 --> 00:04:12.927
But underneath, the coding decisions
changed. Of the codes that at least one

00:04:12.927 --> 00:04:18.572
run applied, 64% for Haiku and 43% for
Luna were not applied by all four runs.

00:04:18.952 --> 00:04:23.399
Two runs can earn the same score while
disagreeing about which codes apply,

00:04:23.399 --> 00:04:27.016
which matters whenever individual
codes are used as evidence.

00:04:28.516 --> 00:04:30.247
Third, the choices interacted.

00:04:30.627 --> 00:04:36.982
Asking the model to decide on every code
raised MCC by 0.10 when the whole codebook

00:04:36.982 --> 00:04:43.030
was sent at once, but by only 0.02 when
the codebook was split into five parts.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.003
So the effect of one choice depends
on the others, and comparing

00:04:47.003 --> 00:04:50.989
models at one fixed setup answers a
narrower question than it seems to.

00:04:52.488 --> 00:04:54.638
Fourth, the human reference
shapes the answer.

00:04:55.018 --> 00:04:57.345
The annotators followed their own rules.

00:04:57.345 --> 00:05:00.777
For example, they skipped each
report's Discussion section,

00:05:00.777 --> 00:05:03.686
while the models read
everything, so some apparent

00:05:03.686 --> 00:05:06.362
AI mistakes were simply
outside the reference.

00:05:06.742 --> 00:05:09.914
How agreement is scored matters too.

00:05:09.914 --> 00:05:14.408
Dividing the codebook
appeared to cost 0.045 in MCC

00:05:14.408 --> 00:05:18.109
across all 94 codes, but only 0.011 across

00:05:18.109 --> 00:05:21.986
the 12 codes the
annotators had prioritized.

00:05:22.366 --> 00:05:26.575
And a different statistic, balanced
accuracy, picked a different setup as best.

00:05:28.075 --> 00:05:32.260
Fifth, model rankings did not
travel. In earlier runs, four models,

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:37.321
including two larger ones, scored almost
identically on the 40 screening reports.

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:40.414
On 50 reports that had been set aside,

00:05:40.414 --> 00:05:43.983
they pulled apart, and
Claude Sonnet rose to 0.64.

00:05:44.363 --> 00:05:47.357
A simple majority vote
across the four did about as

00:05:47.357 --> 00:05:50.232
well as the best of them,
without needing to know

00:05:50.232 --> 00:05:53.284
which model that would be,
although it costs as much

00:05:53.284 --> 00:05:56.219
as all four runs. This
comparison was exploratory.

00:05:57.719 --> 00:06:00.905
Finally, good agreement did
not require an expensive setup.

00:06:01.285 --> 00:06:04.456
One Luna setup came
within 0.01 of the best

00:06:04.456 --> 00:06:07.626
score for about 18 US
cents per 40 reports.

00:06:07.626 --> 00:06:10.870
Earlier runs with larger
models scored about

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.187
the same, at roughly 18
to 28 times the cost.

00:06:15.687 --> 00:06:20.194
For teams using AI-assisted coding,
the results suggest four practices.

00:06:20.574 --> 00:06:23.261
One: report the full
setup, including prompts,

00:06:23.261 --> 00:06:25.831
model versions, settings,
and scoring rules,

00:06:25.831 --> 00:06:30.212
and test whether the results hold when
the most influential choices change.

00:06:30.592 --> 00:06:33.399
Two: repeat at least one
setup, and compare the

00:06:33.399 --> 00:06:36.683
repeats code by code, not
only by their overall scores.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:40.518
Three: where the human reference
covers less than the AI was

00:06:40.518 --> 00:06:44.088
asked to code, also score the
AI on what the reference covers.

00:06:44.468 --> 00:06:47.196
Four: choose the agreement
statistic in advance,

00:06:47.196 --> 00:06:50.720
and pick a setup on some documents,
then confirm it on others.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:56.514
One study on one dataset cannot say
which choices matter in general.

00:06:56.514 --> 00:07:01.692
That needs comparable studies across many
datasets, frameworks, and kinds of text.

00:07:02.072 --> 00:07:05.555
This does not need a coordinated
project, only studies

00:07:05.555 --> 00:07:09.231
that can be compared. The
preprint offers open questions,

00:07:09.231 --> 00:07:11.940
open tools, and a common reporting format,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.939
so any team with coded qualitative
data can run its own study.

00:07:16.319 --> 00:07:21.234
If the same choices matter across datasets,
that could inform default settings and

00:07:21.234 --> 00:07:26.149
reporting guidance. If not, setups should
be tuned and confirmed for each project.

00:07:26.529 --> 00:07:30.542
Before AI-assisted coding can be
relied on as a research instrument,

00:07:30.542 --> 00:07:35.500
we must establish how much its results
depend on the choices made in configuring it.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.636
The write-up, the preprint, and the
open tools are linked on screen.
